Chapter 29 — home renos part 2
The dream dragged him under hard.
Younger. Smaller. Appalachian dark. He was on his back in the mud while something vast and wrong stood over him—too many limbs, a face that wouldn’t hold still. It hit him again. Bone cracked. He couldn’t get up. Then the fire started. It poured off the thing in sheets, catching the trees, racing through the undergrowth. Screams rose. Not human. The cryptids he knew—the ones who had watched him, fed him, kept him alive—were burning. A dullahan’s horse thrashing. Something with antlers collapsing into the flames.
“Stop it!” His voice cracked, younger, raw. “You’re hurting them! I said stop!!!”
He lunged. The thing backhanded him into a trunk. The fire kept spreading. The screams didn’t stop.
Curtis shot awake.
He was upright in the bed, breathing heavy, sweat on his chest. Heterochromia wide in the dark. Hina jerked beside him. Yui was already pushing up on his other side, hand on his arm.
“Curtis?” Hina’s voice was thick with sleep and alarm. “Hey—hey, you’re here.”
He didn’t answer right away. His heart was still slamming. The screams lingered behind his eyes. The low, steady pull in his chest had gone sharp and cold. He stared at the far wall of the apartment, chest rising and falling, while the two girls stayed close and the nightmare slowly let go of his throat.
Curtis dragged a hand down his face. The smirk came easy, practiced. He glanced at the two of them still pressed close in the dark.
“It’s nothing. Just a nightmare. I think I’m homesick.”
Hina’s hand stayed on his arm. She didn’t look convinced. Yui’s fingers were still curled against his ribs. The apartment was quiet except for his breathing finally starting to even out.
“Homesick,” Yui said, soft but pointed. “You woke up like something was burning. That’s not missing the mountains.”
“Same thing some nights.” He leaned back against the headboard, scarred hand resting over Hina’s. “Forest. Things in it. Doesn’t always stay there when I sleep.” The smirk faded into something smaller. “I’m fine. Go back to sleep.”
Hina didn’t move away. She shifted closer instead, head on his shoulder. “We’re already up. You don’t have to do the ‘it’s nothing’ voice.”
Curtis was quiet for a second. The screams had faded. The low, steady pull in his chest was still sharper than usual. He didn’t tell them about the entity or the burning cryptids. He just let them stay where they were, the three of them awake in the dark, while the city outside kept moving and the nightmare sat unspoken between his heartbeat and their hands.
Curtis stared at the ceiling. The smirk was gone. His voice came lower, more even than the nightmare had left him.
“Before I came here, I often wandered through the Appalachian mountains and forests. Eventually the deer ladies, mothmen, sasquatch people, and even the fae opened up to me. I would do my best to deter trespassers or deal with the feral cryptids.” He flexed the scarred hand once. “I’m just a little worried if I’m not there, who’s protecting them?”
Hina’s head stayed on his shoulder. She didn’t interrupt. Yui’s arm tightened across his stomach, the usual edge softened.
“That’s why you keep going into Aokigahara,” Yui said quietly. “It feels like the same job.”
“Close enough.” Curtis exhaled. “Doesn’t have the same faces. Still has things that don’t belong on tourist trails. I can handle those. The ones back home… I left them. That’s the part that sits wrong when I sleep.”
Hina’s fingers traced the scar again, gentle. “You didn’t abandon them. You got sent here. There’s a difference.” She paused. “But I get why it doesn’t feel like one at three in the morning.”
He didn’t answer right away. The low, steady pull in his chest had eased a fraction with the words out. The three of them stayed in the dark, the city quiet beyond the window, while the names of things most people didn’t believe in settled into the room and neither girl told him the worry was stupid.
Morning light was starting to edge the curtains when Curtis sat up. He rubbed his face, the nightmare mostly faded, and swung his legs off the bed.
“I’m going back to my suite.”
Hina caught his wrist before he stood. She was still half under the sheet, hair messy. “Please actually go there and not to the forest.”
He looked at her. The promise came easy. “I promise, Hina.”
Yui, sprawled on his other side, leaned up and kissed the side of his neck, soft and quick. “Good.”
He dressed in the clothes he’d left on the floor, gave them both a short nod, and headed out. The apartment door clicked shut behind him. Downstairs his suite light came on a minute later and stayed on.
Yui sat up, stretched, and padded toward the living room in one of the discarded shirts. She stopped in the doorway. “Hey, Hina? The couch is, well… stained.”
Hina was already in the kitchen, coffee maker running. She glanced over, cheeks pink, and didn’t deny it. “I’ll deal with it after caffeine. Or we throw a blanket on it and pretend we didn’t wreck the thing.”
Yui snorted and dropped onto a kitchen chair. “Blanket. Definitely blanket.”
The coffee finished. They moved around each other in the small kitchen, the night still sitting on their skin, while the suite below stayed lit and the forest, for once, waited until later.
Seven a.m. Yui was on the balcony in the same oversized shirt, coffee in one hand, cigarette in the other. The morning was cool. She watched the side street out of habit.
Curtis stepped out of the building below. Clean clothes. Jacket on. No torn forest gear. He locked the entrance and started down the sidewalk toward the station, hands in his pockets, pace even.
Yui took a drag and called back into the apartment without turning around.
“Hey, Hina. He’s leaving.”
Hina appeared in the balcony doorway a second later, mug in hand, hair still damp from a quick shower. She looked down. Curtis was already half a block away. He hadn’t glanced up. He also hadn’t turned toward the tree line.
“Station direction,” Hina said. “Not the forest.”
“Yet.” Yui exhaled smoke. “He promised you. We’ll see if it holds past noon.”
They watched until he turned the corner. The cigarette burned between Yui’s fingers. Hina stayed in the doorway. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest was quiet somewhere ahead of him, and the two girls on the balcony let him walk while the coffee cooled and the day officially started.
They were still on the balcony an hour later when the truck pulled up. Curtis got out of the passenger side. Two men in work clothes climbed out after him and started unloading—sheets of drywall, boxes of flooring, buckets of compound. He directed them toward the side entrance with short gestures, scarred hand pointing, expression even under the morning light.
Yui leaned on the rail, cigarette forgotten. “What the hell is our forest boy doing?”
Hina stepped up beside her, eyes wide. “He’s… renovating? The suite was already livable.”
“Apparently not enough.” Yui watched him sign something on a clipboard and hand it back. “Drywall and flooring. He’s turning the basement into an actual apartment while we were up here worrying about trees.”
Curtis glanced up once, caught them watching, and gave a short wave before following the workers inside. The truck idled. Materials kept moving. Hina’s mouth curved despite herself.
“He promised he wouldn’t go to the forest. He didn’t say anything about home improvement.”
Yui snorted and finally took the last drag. “Cryptid boyfriend’s redecorating. I’m not even mad.”
They stayed at the rail while the supplies disappeared down the stairs and the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet under the ordinary noise of a delivery.
By late evening the workers were gone. The truck had left. Yui and Hina came down the stairs and stopped in the open doorway of the suite.
The concrete walls were covered. Fresh drywall ran floor to ceiling, taped and mudded, waiting for paint. The floors were no longer bare slab—new laminate ran clean and even under their feet, still smelling faintly of the factory. The space looked bigger. Actual. The landlord stood near the kitchenette in his work trousers, arms crossed, listening.
Curtis was in the middle of the room, compression shirt dusted with compound, pointing at a unit tucked beside the bathroom wall. “And I have a dehumidifier stationed here with its downspout pointing in the drain. Nothing will get gummy. Basement moisture stays handled even in summer.”
The landlord grunted, impressed despite himself. “You did all this in a day.”
“Had help with the heavy lifts.” Curtis wiped his hands on a rag. “Rest was straightforward. It’ll be paint and trim next. Then it’s finished.”
Hina and Yui stepped inside. Yui whistled low. “You turned a basement into a real apartment while we were drinking coffee.”
Curtis glanced over, the corner of his mouth twitching. “Told you I wouldn’t go to the forest. Didn’t say I’d sit still.”
The landlord nodded at the dehumidifier again, then at Curtis. “Keep the receipt for the unit. If it works through rainy season, I’ll knock something off next month’s rent.” He headed for the door, pausing to eye the two girls. “Don’t let him knock down a support wall. I’ll know.”
He left. The suite was quiet except for the low hum of the dehumidifier. Hina ran her fingers along the new wall. Yui dropped onto the edge of the bed frame that had been pushed aside. Curtis stood in the middle of the half-finished room, dust on his clothes, the low, steady pull in his chest quiet, while the place he’d been sleeping in finally started to look like somewhere that could stay.
The landlord’s footsteps faded up the stairs. The dehumidifier hummed. Curtis looked around the half-finished suite—new walls, new floors, the bed frame still shoved to one side—then at the two girls standing in it.
“I liked your guys’ suite,” he said. “So I wanted mine to look more like yours.”
Hina’s expression softened. She stepped closer, fingers still trailing the fresh drywall. “You did all this because our place felt like a real apartment?”
“Yeah.” He shrugged, dust on his shoulder. “Basement was fine. Functional. Yours has walls that aren’t concrete and floors you can walk on without thinking about it. Figured I could get there.” The corner of his mouth twitched. “Still needs paint. And the panels finished. But it’s closer.”
Yui dropped fully onto the bed frame, grinning. “Cryptid boy’s nesting. I’m into it.” She glanced at the dehumidifier. “Also into not living above a mold farm. Good call.”
Hina bumped his arm with hers, warm. “It already looks better. You didn’t have to match us, though.”
“Wanted to.” He met her eyes, easy. “Makes it feel less like I’m just passing through.”
The three of them stood in the improved suite. The city night moved outside the high window. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the new floors settled under their feet and the place started to feel, just a little, like it belonged to someone who planned on staying.
Hina checked the time on her phone and nudged his arm. “C’mon, big guy. Time to wash up and change. I’m sure Janice is going to be at least satisfied that you took it easy instead of training.”
Curtis shook his head, already heading for the bathroom. “I trained. Just not combat training today.”
He stopped by the counter and unbuckled the four cuffs—two from his wrists, two from his ankles. They were thick, black, deceptively simple. He set them down carefully, one by one. The counter creaked under the weight.
Yui leaned over to look. “What, some simple weights? What the hell, how come I can’t—” She tried to lift one wrist cuff with both hands and barely shifted it. Her eyes widened. “Okay, nope.”
“They’re two hundred pounds each.” Curtis flexed his fingers, the skin where the cuffs had sat a little marked. “Worn all day. Walking, carrying drywall, whatever. Keeps the baseline up without having to go find something that hits back.”
Hina stared at the cuffs, then at him. “You’ve been wearing eight hundred pounds since this morning.”
“Since I left.” He grabbed a clean shirt from the duffel. “Janice gets a face that isn’t split. I still get the work in. Fair trade.”
Yui let go of the cuff like it might bite. “You’re ridiculous. Also, wash the drywall dust off before you put that shirt on. We’re not explaining white handprints to the club.”
Curtis huffed a short laugh and headed for the shower. The cuffs stayed on the counter, heavy and ordinary-looking, while the two girls looked at each other and the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet under the sound of the water starting.
